This crate (`symphonia-bundle-shorten`) is a pure-Rust Shorten (`.shn`) lossless audio demuxer and
decoder. It is licensed under the Mozilla Public License, v. 2.0, like the rest of Project Symphonia
(see `LICENSE`).

Algorithms derived from FFmpeg
------------------------------

The decoder is a port of the algorithm of FFmpeg's `libavcodec/shorten.c` (the command grammar, the
Rice/Golomb residual codes, the fixed polynomial and quantized LPC predictors, the running mean
offset, the bit shift and the 32-bit wrapping arithmetic) and the stream header parsing follows
`libavcodec/shorten.c` (`read_header`) and `libavformat/shortendec.c`, so that its output is bit
exact with FFmpeg's. FFmpeg is licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or
later; its copyright notices follow:

    Copyright (c) 2005 Jeff Muizelaar
    Copyright (c) 2001 Fabrice Bellard
    Copyright (c) 2005 Alex Beregszaszi
    Copyright (c) 2015 Carl Eugen Hoyos

No FFmpeg source code is copied; the Rust code is an independent reimplementation. Anybody
redistributing this crate should therefore treat the algorithm-level derivation above as subject to
the LGPL-2.1-or-later terms in addition to the MPL-2.0 terms of the crate itself.

Format references
-----------------

* T. Robinson, "SHORTEN: Simple lossless and near-lossless waveform compression", Cambridge
  University Engineering Department technical report CUED/F-INFENG/TR.156, 1994, describes the
  coding scheme.
* The file type numbering, the command codes, the field widths and the layout of the seek table
  trailer (`SEEK`, `SHNAMPSK`) are facts of the file format taken from the header files and the
  documentation of the Shorten 3.6.1 reference implementation by Tony Robinson (SoftSound).
  Its source code was used only to read the format and to generate test streams (with the types
  that FFmpeg does not decode); none of it is copied into this crate.
* The extended types that FFmpeg rejects (signed 8-bit and unsigned 16-bit samples) follow the
  reference implementation. Streams with u-law or A-law samples are not supported.
